so generally  a potential idea to reduce sample  complexity  that's relevant to the disease will help  to reduce sample complexity  going into the mass spec and that will  improve the quantitative robustness  of your entire workflow  and so now come to the question of  study design so again  if one has unlimited funds  to run a lot of studies then one could  consider  analyzing deep proteome for all samples  for all cohorts  however this often is not practicable in  terms of  how long the mass spec will be  maintaining the  standard of instrument that we would  like  to have for the for the analysis  as well as in terms of the cost  so in  instead of doing the rectangular design  which is shown on the left of this slide  which is very often used for genomic  studies proteomics studies have often  used  the triangular design which is shown on  the right side of the study  and in this design there are different  phases of study starting from the top  of the pyramid where the number of  samples  so the cohorts are starts with a small  cohort so in this step one would  carefully select  and match the samples the subjects  and then one may use  a dia or cda method  to measure as many proteins as possible  and i would still apply for a sub  proteome approach if possible and  after that a set of candidates are  selected through the statistical  analysis  then a validation or verification cohort  or qualification cohort different terms  have been applied to this  is then used as an intermediate step and  often  one might design a mrm assay to very  accurately quantitate  the small subset of candidates  and this can then lead to an even  smaller subset although  environment assays could measure  around 50  or tens of proteins so it may not need  to really go into an antibody based  stage  towards the end so  faced study design is um quite feasible  in terms of cost and  allows a different selection and  different type  of proteomic methods during the steps  and so overall what this side is  summarizing now  the different steps that one might  consider at each  of the stages or each of the workflow  during a clinical proteomics research  project  so in terms of the clinical research  sampling  design the studying for a focused  question and  if possible using longitudinal sampling  from the sun set of subjects  is quite useful because each subject  will provide multiple samples and  act as their own control which takes  away some of the biological  variability across people  we can consider stage relevant sample  selection if the triangular design is  used  so for that what would one would do  is to use  a well-matched sample at the start  and then going to broader selection of  subjects  for the later stages and again cannot  emphasize  enough the standard operating procedure  for sample collection  processing and storage uh in terms of  the laboratory analysis aspect  i would suggest to target a sub proteome  if possible  include internal standing as a qc  use liquid handler for sample processing  and  a very good practice um which i've  learned  during our research is to actually  run through the entire procedure that  has been established  using for example a single sample would  run a few times  to determine the robustness or  variability  of the entire procedure before starting  the whole cohort  that way you know what is the  variability  that occurs just through the sample  processing  then you can be more confident of your  result at the end  so finally in the data analysis analysis  aspect  you can definitely check um  your whole data set initially for  overall quality and if there are any  outliers either due to technical  reason or a biological reason um those  outliers  should be removed from further analysis  the mass spec stability across long  across large cohorts definitely should  also be analyzed  so usually there should be mass spec qc  samples for example  often used hela digest for example or a  bsa  digest just  inserted during your sample sequence to  ensure the mathematic  performance is the same across weeks  or months of run for your entire cohort  normalization can be used  as well and finally one should apply a  suitable  statistical analysis to answer the  clinical questions  so now finally i just  want to share a practical example from  our research  so the example i'd like to share with  you is  our discovery of serum protein  biomarkers  for early detection of esophageal  adenocarcinoma  now this is the cancer that develops at  the lower esophagus  most patients that suffer from this  disease are diagnosed at late incurable  stages  leading to a median survival rate of  less than one year  there is a recognized pre-malignant  condition called  barrett's esophagus and the disease  progression then usually occurs  through various oesophagus to high grade  dysplasia which is intermediate stage  and then progressing to esophageal  adenocarcinoma  the whole procedure can be up to 10  years  there are also recognized risk factors  including reflux disease  and males also obesity  are strong risk factors for this disease  now one of the clinical needs is to  develop a more effective diagnostic  test for surveillance or screening  currently the surveillance of various  esophagus patients are done  by endoscopy with biopsy and this is an  invasive procedure and  health economics studies have shown that  the procedure  is not cost effective because of the  load progression rate  and the long time required for disease  progression  so there's the need for biomarkers which  can  detect early disease meaning high grade  dysplasia  stage and for the test to be  less invasive so we aimed to  discover serum protein biomarkers for  this  application and we wanted to discover  markers that can pick up  a high grade dysplasia rather than a  late stage  cancer so to cut a long story short  we decided to focus on glycoproteins  because there's a  literature suggesting that glycosylation  is changed  in during cancer development in general  and during esophageal adenocancer  adenocarcinoma development in particular  and what we decided to do was to set up  a  high throughput screening method using a  liquid handler  and in this method we use individual  lectins  each coupled to a magnetic bead an array  of  individual lectins coated with beads on  the 96 well  lectins are natural proteins which  their job is to find different types of  glycosylation glycosylated proteins  so we use it as a tool to isolate  different types of glycosylated  proteins this has been used  quite a lot in glycobiology the  difference with our method  is that we use individual lectins and we  initially selected  20 different lectins to use to screen  against one serum  and in this method then the  individual serum samples are  denatured and we added a chicken of  albumen to each  serum sample at the start as an internal  standard so we can monitor  the entire workflow by  chicken of albumin peptides which are  not in any of our samples  so after that the denatured proteins  are captured at the protein level with  the  individual lectin bees on the 96 well  the wash proteins the unbound proteins  are washed away  and then we did on bead tryptic digest  procedure  which then we can collect the  peptides from the supernatant for mass  spec analysis  and the entire workflow was set up on a  bravo liquid handler  platform so  the study cohorts were used so we  chose a triangular workflow  and initially in the first stage we had  10 patients per group and we analyzed  healthy the barriers of esophagus and  the cancer group and  we used dda acquisition method  and we had 20 different lectins here so  we screened  a large number of proteins in this  method  from there we chose six lectins as the  most promising  and designed mrm against  around 50 proteins which came out as  candidates  and in the second phase we analyzed 20  patients per group  and the groups are again the same as the  first face healthy  varys oso vigorous and cancer and from  the qualification phase we chose  four lectins which were the most  promising and we actually just used the  same mrm assay  but this time we included a um  labeled peptide to some of our top  candidates  to increase the robustness of the meshes  and in the final phase we measured a  large  broad range of samples from an  australian cohort as well as from  a small cohort from the us and  at this stage we added the  dysplasia samples so low grade and high  grade dyslexia  and the goal here is to find define a  biomarker signature which can  detect high grade dysplasia and cancer  labelled in red here so these are the  treatment the  groups that will receive treatment and  then we  want to distinguish this group from the  healthy baristas objects and low-grade  dysplasia  now the results were of all these stages  were published in these two studies  the final outcome is we found a 10  glycoprotein biomarker  panel which can detect high grade  dysplasia and esophageal cancer as  a group from the other group that don't  need treatment  and the specificity for in the final  stage we divided our  cohort into a training cohort which was  used to develop this 10 marker panel  which has a specificity and sensitivity  over 85  then we used that biomarker panel to  test  the usa cohort and the sensitivity and  specificity as  expected in this scenario was lower than  the  training cohort but it's still fairly  reasonable and this is a quite promising  result from these different cohorts  so finally um just reminds me to  summarize what we went through  today so we  consider the key  aspects of variability in proteomic  research using clinical cohorts and  some practical solutions that could  achieve robustness  in the quantitative proteomics analysis  of large clinical cohorts  and then i gave you an example of what  how we  discovered serum glycoprotein biomarkers  for early  esophageal adenocarcinoma and  that involves the use of magnetic b  coupled  lectins and which we then developed  within multiple reaction monitoring  assay  allowing validation in clinical cohorts  of several hundred samples over the  three months run we  achieved excellent reproducibility  for the spiked in standard so we were  quite confident of the result  so now we're looking at further  developing the assay  for clinical use in the future  so thank you very much for joining me  today and hopefully  there are some tips that you have  learned from today's webinar  and i'd be very happy to answer any  questions  you dr hill for your informative  presentation now as you mentioned we  will now start the live q a portion of  our webinar  to our audience if you have any  questions you would like to ask please  do so now  just click on that ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  and we will answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  okay let's get started we have some  great questions already coming in  dr hill let's start with this one should  one be concerned about batch effects  in clinical proteomics research  thank you for that that's a very good  question and the answer is  yes so one  thing that i actually neglected to  mention and yes thank you for the  question  is that the um the cohorts the samples  should be randomized  during the run so that means um the  for example the cancer and the  non-cancer samples  should be mixed in order so one should  not run  or sample all cancer samples at the  start and all non-cancer samples at the  end  and in terms of the batch effect that's  one of the reasons of batch effect if  you are running autumn  for example group a at the start when  the mass spec is in very good condition  and then you see a drop in mass effect  performance to  during the end of the run when group b  are all run  and that's one of the reasons that one  should randomize samples  and the second reason a second way to  randomize is if you have multiple  micro plates for example or different  batches of processing then one should  definitely randomize the samples across  different plates or batches of  processing and that means  group a and group b should be in both  plates or batches  thank you dr hill now this next question  is two parts  do you have any recommendations for  cohort size and selection for biomarker  studies  and should samples be matched for  clinical parameters across study groups  thank you for the question um so  the cohort size is  slightly variable so um  it depends again on the number available  and the study design that you go with  so i can comment more on the triangular  study design which is what we have done  so you would have seen in our initial  study  in the first phase we just did 10  samples per group  in later studies we've moved to 20  samples per group  although the 10 samples per group gave  good results we  wanted to use more and we had the budget  to do more in later studies  so probably around yeah 15 to 20 at the  initial  stage is good quite often  a sample size calculation may be asked  for when you for example do grant  applications  however if you don't have any data it's  just a discovery stage  you really cannot do a sample size  calculation  um accurately as such so  say yeah i would say yeah between 10 and  20 per group is a good  size to start with for selection in the  initial  phase the discovery stage i would  recommend  to match samples very well between your  groups so group a and b  definitely should be age and sex matched  and  any other potential confounding factors  it's  possibly good to match them so you get  very homogeneous results  and you get candidates going forward to  the next phases  for next phases then i would relax  the sample matching criteria so you can  really observe how the candidates are  performing  across the different types of samples  that you have  thank you dr hill now we have time for a  few more questions  spiking in an internal standard protein  is a good idea  should that protein be used to normalize  normalized samples  such as one would use actin for western  blots  um yeah that's a good question so yeah  indeed yeah i like  to add in an internal standard protein  to  check the quality of the workflow  for each of the samples  particularly when you have a cohort of a  few hundred  even using liquid handler occasionally  there may be some samples that just did  not get a good quality  processing and then one could see  that the internal standard um peptides  that were generated from the spiked in  protein so which we're using  um albumin chicken of argument in the  example i gave  so um the use of  that for normalization is  um good when the samples are not  too variable so if it's clear  that one of the samples there was a  problem with the processing and for  example you hardly see  any of your internal standard peptide  then i would actually  remove that sample from the rest of the  analysis  on the basis that something went wrong  during the processing and the data is  is low quality rather than normalized  if there's only small variability  between  samples for the internal standard  protein then yes indeed you can use  this internal standard protein for  normalization  that would be my suggestion  thank you dr hill we have time for one  more question with the invention of data  independent  acquisition will shotgun proteomics and  targeted proteomics be abandoned  in the future  that's a good question so um the dia  acquisition um has been used in few  studies now  it has some advantages and i think we  are still  learning uh on all of the details on how  it works  so at the moment the other two  approaches are still used quite a lot  the mrn assay may  not probably will not be abandoned  because  in the clinical laboratories  mrm assays are used to measure  metabolites and so it's a workflow  that the clinical labs are already used  for diagnostic  purposes so in terms of translating  clinical proteomics research to clinical  assays and the mrm assay is a very good  bridge  so i would think that  using mrosa as that link between  research and implementation would be a  good idea and that's  in fact what we are aiming to do with  our essay  so thank you for that question  and thank you dr hill i'd also like to  thank our audience  for joining us today and for their  interesting questions  those questions we were unable to answer  today and those that are submitted  during the on-demand period  they will be addressed by dr hill via  the email address you provided at the  time of registration  i would once again like to thank dr  michelle hill for her time today  and for her important research we'd also  like to thank labyrus  and our sponsor agilent for underwriting  today's educational webcast  today's presentation is educational and  it does offer continuing education  credits  if you are interested in receiving your  credits please click on the continuing  education credits tab  located at the top right of the  presentation window and follow the  process to obtain your credits  this webcast can be viewed on demand and  you may continue to submit questions  during the on-demand period  labrets will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  and we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event that's all for now  and we hope to see you again soon  have a great day
 

